 "signs": [
  {
    "x": 28,
    "y": 56,
    "plaque": "arc_england_bowden_bridge",
    "title": "Bowden Bridge Quarry, 1932",
    "text": "On 24 April 1932 the walkers of the Kinder trespass gathered at Bowden Bridge quarry near Hayfield before they climbed. The mass trespass is remembered as the spark for the 1949 National Parks and Access to the Countryside Act, and for the Pennine Way, which opened in 1965. A plaque marks the spot."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 59,
   "text": "GOYT VALLEY. Pay and display. The honesty box is not an honesty box, it is a ticket machine."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 54,
   "text": "ERRWOOD RESERVOIR, built 1967 to quench Stockport. The old road under the water is still down there."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "ERRWOOD HALL. Once grand, now rhododendrons. They always win in the end."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "NORTH: Manchester, 20 miles. Rain: 20 miles of it."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "SHINING TOR, 559 metres. Highest point in Cheshire. Say it loudly, they will only half believe you."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "THE SPANISH SHRINE. A family built it for their governess. Best staff benefits in the Peak."
  },
  {
   "x": 43,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "FERNILEE RESERVOIR, 1938. Dam, dignity and a lot of heron."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 10,
   "plaque": "goyt_valley_national_park",
   "title": "The First National Park",
   "text": "In 1951 the Peak District became Britain's very first national park, after years of campaigning by walkers, including the famous mass trespass on Kinder Scout in 1932. The idea was simple: these hills belong to everyone. Shut the gate behind you, though."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 50,
   "plaque": "goyt_valley_mersey",
   "title": "The Goyt and the Mersey",
   "text": "The River Goyt rises on the moors above here and runs north to Stockport, where it meets the Tame and together they become the Mersey. Mersey is Old English for 'boundary river': it once marked the edge between the kingdoms of Mercia and Northumbria."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 9,
   "plaque": "landsky_goyt_carbon",
   "title": "Peat and carbon",
   "text": "A bog grows about a millimetre a year, as sphagnum moss dies and piles up too wet to rot. All the carbon the moss took from the air stays locked in the peat, and the peatlands of the United Kingdom hold about three billion tonnes of it, more than all its woods. Drain a bog and the peat dries, shrinks and gives the carbon back; most of ours has been drained, burnt or dug. Rewet it and the moss returns, the floods slow and the carbon stays put."
  }
 ],
